//! stage:typing no-std
// attack (review): capture, with a requirement that mentions the function's parameter (the design's
// assumption A7 said this needs a requirement without parameters). The recursive call f<T, W<T.X>>
// instantiates T at itself. Must be rejected: 'W<T.X> is R' is not known in the body of f. With
// assocsubst the blanket conformance gives T.X = B and the call is accepted through it.
// expected (premsubst with the capture rule of 3.4): rejected, with and without assocsubst.
// observed: (eq25, premsubst as first designed) FULL+premsubst rejected (bound=1 released=4); +selfcheck same; +assocsubst accepted; NEW accepted
// currently: base rejected (subgoals=9 matches=689): no coercion from '(P<T>, Q<(T::P).X>, R<W<(T::P).X>>) ==> [Void]() let -> Void' to '[Void]() let -> Void'; FULL rejected (subgoals=5 matches=85): no coercion from '(P<T>, Q<(T::P).X>, R<W<(T::P).X>>) ==> [Void]() let -> Void' to '[Void]() let -> Void'
trait P { type X }
trait Q {}
trait R {}
struct B {}
struct W<T> {}
given B is Q {}
given W<B> is R {}
given <T> => T is P { type X = B }
fun f<T, V where T is P, T.X is Q, V is R>() {
  f<T, W<T.X>>()
}
